  "reasoning": "The claim that lightning never strikes the same place twice is a widespread myth and scientifically false. According to the National Weather Service (NWS) and Britannica, lightning frequently strikes the exact same location multiple times, particularly tall, pointed, or isolated structures that offer a path of least resistance for electrical discharge. For instance, the NWS notes that the Empire State Building is struck by lightning an average of 23 times each year. While the phrase is commonly used as an idiom meaning an unusual event is unlikely to recur, it is physically inaccurate.",
